WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:01.470
Hi!

00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:06.890
Large language models, or short LLMs, are
powerful but often slow at inference.

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:13.660
Today we discuss this new method that takes
a pretrained transformer LLM during inference

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:18.600
and it makes it faster and use less memory,
with sparsity.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:23.260
Sparsity means that the transformer only uses
a fraction of its neurons and attention heads

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:24.680
on each input.

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:30.160
This method that makes sparsity work at inference,
is introduced in the Deja Vu paper.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:35.989
It is not easy to make an LLM sparse only
after training, so at inference.

00:00:35.989 --> 00:00:39.260
But if you watch this video, you will learn
why it is hard.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:44.809
Also in this video, you will also learn that
the method is actually quite elegant and simple,

00:00:44.809 --> 00:00:49.920
but you will also learn about
other nuggets of insight about LLMs and transformers

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:56.100
that the DejaVu paper contains, namely how
attention works and what it does to its inputs,

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:01.739
and why MLP layers in transformers usually
have sparse outputs.

00:01:01.739 --> 00:01:07.479
This is quite a technical video towards the
end, so if you want to refresh your knowledge

00:01:07.479 --> 00:01:13.509
about the transformer, please watch our latest
video explaining all transformer components

00:01:13.509 --> 00:01:16.259
such as attention and the MLPs.

00:01:16.259 --> 00:01:22.740
Let’s dive into the Déjà vu paper in this
AI Coffee Break!

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:25.660
Transformer-based LLMs are slow at inference
time.

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:31.159
This directly affects the speed at which your
favorite chatbot responds to you.

00:01:31.159 --> 00:01:34.979
Have you ever wondered what goes on under
the hood when you ask a digital assistant

00:01:34.979 --> 00:01:37.530
a question and why they need time to respond?

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:43.200
It’s because they do a lot of computations
given an input, they pass it through the self-attention

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:46.130
layer,
which scales quadratically with sequence length

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:48.630
and consists of multiple attention heads.

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:52.420
Watch out last video if you need a refresher
on transformers!

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:56.200
Then the input passes
through the MLP layer, which is a feedforward

00:01:56.200 --> 00:02:01.189
network of usually a layer doubling the size
of the input embedding and one reducing it

00:02:01.189 --> 00:02:03.979
back to the size of the input embedding.

00:02:03.979 --> 00:02:08.550
And that was just one transformer layer:
The process repeats depending on how many

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:15.460
layers the LLM has, which could be 6 or 12,
or 48, or a thousand.

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:19.690
Larger models with more layers, can be more
powerful because they have more time to transform

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:22.520
the inputs and shape them into the right answer.

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:25.640
But more layers are more demanding in terms
of computations.

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:32.700
Furthermore, LLMs also spend significant time
loading all parameters into the memory of

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:33.920
your device.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:38.940
Imagine you have a transformer-based app on
your phone, which you open, type in a query,

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:42.110
get your answer and quit the app.

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:46.700
You would become quickly frustrated if the
app needed to load for a long time when you

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:49.409
are just wanting to quickly ask it something.

00:02:49.409 --> 00:02:55.530
So, how can we make these powerful tools more
efficient and user-friendly?

00:02:55.530 --> 00:03:00.000
This is where the innovations in the Deja
Vu paper come in - they aim to reduce these

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.010
loading times significantly by making the
LLM sparse, meaning that Déjà vu reduces

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:08.769
the number of parameters one needs to load
and activate.

00:03:08.769 --> 00:03:13.129
But the idea of sparse neural networks, where
only a small fraction of the model parameters

00:03:13.129 --> 00:03:17.709
activates, such as 5% or 10%
goes back to 1989.

00:03:17.709 --> 00:03:22.209
In theory, sparsity could save up all the
computations that the turned off neurons do

00:03:22.209 --> 00:03:25.851
not have to do anymore,
and also, it would speed up the loading time

00:03:25.851 --> 00:03:29.620
of the model, since you do not need to load
all the unused weights.

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:34.459
But the problem with modern hardware, such
as the GPUs on which we usually run LLMs,

00:03:34.459 --> 00:03:40.810
is that they are optimized for dense networks
whose layers are described by dense matrices

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:43.060
without a lot of zeros.

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:48.290
Sparse networks are described by sparse matrices
with a lot of zeros in them, and since GPUs

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:53.549
are not optimized for sparse matrix multiplications,
sparsifying the network does not actually

00:03:53.549 --> 00:03:55.650
give us the speedup we'd expect.

00:03:55.650 --> 00:04:00.060
Also, to make a sparse neural network run
accurately, one needs to train it this way

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:05.629
to “prepare” it so to say for the fact
that some components will turn off at inference

00:04:05.629 --> 00:04:06.629
time.

00:04:06.629 --> 00:04:12.790
And since most LLMs are dense, one would need
expensive retraining to sparsify them.

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:18.350
Another problem arises when sparsity takes
a static pattern: It turns out that if LLMs

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:24.710
are using static sparsity, meaning that we
turn off neurons independently of the input,

00:04:24.710 --> 00:04:27.919
they lose their ability to perform in-context
learning.

00:04:27.919 --> 00:04:33.280
Which is not unexpected since in-context learning
is all about attending to user-given examples

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:34.389
in the input.

00:04:34.389 --> 00:04:40.250
Turning off attention heads and neurons independently
of the input, might ruin the LLM’s attention

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:42.669
to the examples given by the user.

00:04:42.669 --> 00:04:47.630
Therefore, the idea of the Deja Vu paper is
to turn off neurons and attention heads depending

00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:52.260
on the input, which the authors call contextual
sparsity.

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:56.370
Contextual sparsity means the we dynamically
decide which parts to use based on the specific

00:04:56.370 --> 00:05:01.480
current input, much like how your brain only
activates certain parts when solving different

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:02.509
types of problems.

00:05:02.509 --> 00:05:09.070
To figure out which neurons and attention
heads to use and which ones to turn off, DEJAVU

00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:12.810
employs simpler neural networks.

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:18.500
The authors of Déjà vu do the theoretical
work to motivate contextual sparsity, and

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:20.540
do and make empirical analyses.

00:05:20.540 --> 00:05:27.270
From the list of authors, you maybe know Tri
Dao for his influential work on Flash Attention,

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:31.520
that has been adopted very fast after release.

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:36.620
With Tri Dao contributing to the paper, you
can be sure that DEJAVU is all about making

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:39.370
transformers work faster!

00:05:39.370 --> 00:05:44.600
To understand how many of the attention heads
and how many neurons in the MLP layers can

00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:49.400
be deleted without ruining performance,
the authors conducted an experiment using

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:52.819
the OpenBookQA and Wiki-Text datasets.

00:05:52.819 --> 00:05:58.260
The authors take input samples from those
datasets and ran them through OPT of various

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:01.650
sizes (30 billion, 66 and 175 billion parameters).

00:06:01.650 --> 00:06:11.050
They recorded which attention heads and which
MLP neurons had outputs with large norm.

00:06:11.050 --> 00:06:18.789
Then with the same input, they ran the LLMs
again but turned off 80% of the attention

00:06:18.789 --> 00:06:24.860
heads with smallest norm and 95% of the MLP
neurons with smallest norm.

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:28.610
They saw that on average the performance did
not degrade.

00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:34.840
This suggests that a large part of the model
may not be essential for its overall functionality,

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:40.660
paving the way for developing DEJAVU to cut
off the unneeded components.

00:06:40.660 --> 00:06:47.500
So for DEJAVU, they used the recorded pairs
of input and large or small norms and trained

00:06:47.500 --> 00:06:54.270
for each MLP layer a simple fully connected
neural network with two layers to classify

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:57.630
which of the MLP neurons has large norm.

00:06:57.630 --> 00:07:02.790
They also trained the same MLP architecture
for each attention layer to predict which

00:07:02.790 --> 00:07:05.550
of the attention heads has high norm.

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:12.190
Then to sparsify the LLM for any inputs, they
let it run only with the neurons and attention

00:07:12.190 --> 00:07:15.840
heads predicted by the small neural networks.

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:22.580
But how does it come that the time these small
sparsity predictor networks need to run their

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:28.650
decisions, does not exceed the time the layer
would have done that computation in the first

00:07:28.650 --> 00:07:29.650
place?

00:07:29.650 --> 00:07:34.599
Well, it’s because the authors executed
the sparsity prediction for the MLP while

00:07:34.599 --> 00:07:39.039
the attention layers were still computing
their outputs in parallel.

00:07:39.039 --> 00:07:44.300
They implemented DEJAVU mostly in Python with
a necessary implementation of hardware-aware

00:07:44.300 --> 00:07:50.970
sparse matrix multiplication, because remember:
GPUs are fast at dense matrix multiplication

00:07:50.970 --> 00:07:55.510
and they needed this additional programming
to make sparse matrix multiplication actually

00:07:55.510 --> 00:07:57.110
faster than dense matrix multiplication.

00:07:57.110 --> 00:08:04.860
And the whole work paid off, since the authors
could reduce the runtime cost of OPT-175B.

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:11.979
A 75% sparse version of OPT maintained the
same accuracy as the dense OPT on language

00:08:11.979 --> 00:08:18.800
modelling on WikiText and C4 and only after
75% sparsity, performance degraded so perplexity

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:20.180
increased.

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:23.659
It was also successful on downstream task
datasets.

00:08:23.659 --> 00:08:29.370
And DEJAVU was 2 times faster than the FasterTransformer
implementation from NVIDIA which is written

00:08:29.370 --> 00:08:31.729
entirely in C++ and CUDA.

00:08:31.729 --> 00:08:36.909
It was 6 times faster than the most popular
transformer implementation in Huggingface.

00:08:36.909 --> 00:08:40.860
This is great news when trying to make LLM
inference run in real time.

00:08:40.860 --> 00:08:46.190
While the authors only applied DEJAVU to LLMs,
I see no reason why it would not work for

00:08:46.190 --> 00:08:50.870
vision transformer as well, or for transformers
working with other modalities.

00:08:50.870 --> 00:08:58.760
Also, it is noticeable how DEJAVU is in spirit
a kind of Mixture of Experts (MoE) architecture:

00:08:58.760 --> 00:09:04.660
MoE does the following at each layer: it uses
a neural network layer after the attention

00:09:04.660 --> 00:09:11.190
layer to tell which MLP layer to use from
a series of many MLP layers.

00:09:11.190 --> 00:09:16.769
DEJAVU on the other hand, decides with small
neural networks to which neurons from the

00:09:16.769 --> 00:09:21.440
MLP layer and to which attention heads to
route the input to.

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:27.610
So DEJAVU works on neuron and attention level
and at inference time, while MoE already applies

00:09:27.610 --> 00:09:35.510
during training and works at the coarser level
of choosing between MLP variants.

00:09:35.510 --> 00:09:40.220
This was the method, but the paper still has
theoretical insights to offer.

00:09:40.220 --> 00:09:44.330
Did you wonder
why one can get away with using just 20% of

00:09:44.330 --> 00:09:49.690
attention heads with the highest norm and
just 5% of MLP neurons with highest norm without

00:09:49.690 --> 00:09:51.030
losing performance?

00:09:51.030 --> 00:09:56.480
Well, the authors point out that contextual
sparsity in MLPs comes because the ReLU or

00:09:56.480 --> 00:10:00.730
GeLU activation functions set all negative
activations to zero anyway.

00:10:00.730 --> 00:10:04.839
Furthermore, the authors empirically observe
that the cosine similarity is high between

00:10:04.839 --> 00:10:09.640
representations from one layer to the next,
meaning that the activations from layer to

00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:11.839
layer do not change a lot.

00:10:11.839 --> 00:10:16.730
This is because there is a residual connection
around the attention block and one around

00:10:16.730 --> 00:10:18.269
the MLP block.

00:10:18.269 --> 00:10:21.090
Each layer learns to add only a small norm.

00:10:21.090 --> 00:10:24.160
In comparison, the norm of the residual connection
is large.

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:29.320
But why is the norm of the layer small while
the norm of the residual is high? because

00:10:29.320 --> 00:10:35.150
most of the vector values of the activations
in the MLP are zero, because of ReLU and GeLU

00:10:35.150 --> 00:10:36.150
activations.

00:10:36.150 --> 00:10:40.279
But why does contextual sparsity exist also
for attention?

00:10:40.279 --> 00:10:46.010
We have known from previous research on attention
head pruning, that one can get rid of the

00:10:46.010 --> 00:10:51.630
same attention heads for all input samples,
because some attention heads turn out to be

00:10:51.630 --> 00:10:52.970
useless on average.

00:10:52.970 --> 00:10:58.300
But contextual sparsity is different, because
it means that some inputs need some attention

00:10:58.300 --> 00:11:08.170
heads, while other inputs need other attention
heads.

00:11:08.170 --> 00:11:12.670
When trying to understand contextual sparsity
in attention blocks, the authors see that

00:11:12.670 --> 00:11:18.490
there are heads with uniform attention scores
and “heavy hitter” heads with high attention

00:11:18.490 --> 00:11:20.459
values on some tokens.

00:11:20.459 --> 00:11:27.220
They observe how it is important to keep the
heavy hitters since they are responsible for

00:11:27.220 --> 00:11:29.410
the interesting token interactions.

00:11:29.410 --> 00:11:33.880
But why are heavy hitters modelling interesting
interactions and the uniform ones don’t?

00:11:33.880 --> 00:11:37.440
Well, the explanation is kind of lengthy,
so sit down.

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:42.920
It’s because attention might be performing
something similar to mean-shift clustering.

00:11:42.920 --> 00:11:48.690
Ms. Coffee Bean, was it mean-shift clustering?

00:11:48.690 --> 00:11:53.269
Mean-shift clustering works by computing centroids
for each cluster.

00:11:53.269 --> 00:11:57.670
It starts with random centroids and updates
the centroids for each cluster by calculating

00:11:57.670 --> 00:12:00.709
the mean of the points lying in a certain
region.

00:12:00.709 --> 00:12:04.910
And the mean is adding the points and dividing
by how many they are.

00:12:04.910 --> 00:12:10.180
And attention does something similar: there
we add the value vectors weighted by how similar

00:12:10.180 --> 00:12:12.500
their query and key was.

00:12:12.500 --> 00:12:14.610
So here in attention we add value vectors,

00:12:14.610 --> 00:12:20.899
just like in mean-shift clustering, we add
data points to compute the mean (so the centroid).

00:12:20.899 --> 00:12:25.430
The denser a region, the more data points
contribute to that mean, which is similar

00:12:25.430 --> 00:12:30.070
to high attention scores that make a certain
input token contribute a lot.

00:12:30.070 --> 00:12:32.839
So in mean-shift clustering dense regions
get richer.

00:12:32.839 --> 00:12:37.649
Similarly, in attention, similar tokens get
more similar and get higher and higher attention

00:12:37.649 --> 00:12:40.389
scores (and form these heavy hitters).

00:12:40.389 --> 00:12:46.940
In summary, each self-attention head does
one mean-shift clustering step to push input

00:12:46.940 --> 00:12:52.010
embeddings of tokens together, and we get
heavy-hitter attention heads.

00:12:52.010 --> 00:12:55.940
Wow, you made it until the very end of the
explanation!

00:12:55.940 --> 00:13:00.010
Thanks for watching and let us know in the
comments what you think.

00:13:00.010 --> 00:13:03.450
If you want to see more videos like this one,
subscribe to the channel.

00:13:03.450 --> 00:13:10.010
See you next time!

00:13:10.010 --> 00:13:13.290
Okay, bye!

